latest_plan = 'Based on our reflection, here\'s a detailed step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Analyze the Input Grid:\n   a. Count color frequencies for the entire 10x10 grid.\n   b. Count color frequencies for each 5x5 quadrant.\n   c. Count color frequencies for each 2x2 block.\n\n2. Calculate Color Importance:\n   a. Assign a base importance to each color (e.g., lower for black, higher for others).\n   b. Adjust importance based on global frequency (more frequent = more important).\n   c. Calculate a "distinctiveness" score for each color in each quadrant (how much it stands out).\n\n3. Create a Color Influence Map:\n   a. For each input cell, calculate its influence on the 3x3 area of the output grid it corresponds to.\n   b. Use a gaussian-like distribution for influence (stronger in the center, weaker at edges).\n\n4. Generate the Initial Output Grid:\n   a. For each cell in the 5x5 output:\n      - Consider the corresponding 2x2 input block.\n      - Calculate a score for each color based on:\n        * Local frequency in the 2x2 block\n        * Quadrant frequency\n        * Global frequency\n        * Color importance\n        * Influence from neighboring cells (from the influence map)\n      - Choose the color with the highest score.\n\n5. Apply Context-Aware Adjustments:\n   a. For each cell in the output:\n      - If it\'s black, check if it should be replaced based on neighboring colors and input context.\n      - If it\'s an isolated color, consider blending it with neighbors.\n   b. Pay special attention to corners and edges, giving extra weight to the corresponding input areas.\n\n6. Implement Color Transition and Mixing:\n   a. Identify significant color boundaries in the input.\n   b. Ensure these transitions are represented in the output, possibly by introducing intermediate colors.\n\n7. Balance Check and Adjustment:\n   a. Compare the color distribution of the output with the input.\n   b. Identify underrepresented significant colors.\n   c. Selectively adjust cells to better match the input distribution, focusing on areas where the color was prominent in the input.\n\n8. Introduce Controlled Randomness:\n   a. With a small probability, allow some cells to be different from their calculated "best" color.\n   b. Choose the alternative color based on the next highest scoring colors for that cell.\n\n9. Final Coherence Check:\n   a. Ensure no unintended patterns have been introduced.\n   b. Make minor adjustments to improve overall visual coherence if necessary.\n\n10. Validate the Output:\n    a. Check that the 5x5 grid maintains the essential character of the 10x10 input.\n    b. Verify that all significant color patterns from the input are represented.\n    c. Ensure a good balance between color preservation and pattern representation.\n\nThis plan aims to create an output that captures the essence of the input grid while allowing for natural color mixing and transition. It balances local and global color information, accounts for the importance of different colors in context, and ensures that the output is a holistic representation of the input.'
